  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Solve the equation $\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x=0$ for ${0}^{\u2218}\\le x<{360}^{\u2218}.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x \\\\ 1 & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}\u2061x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}\u2061(1)={90}^{\u2218}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${90}^{\u2218}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{\u2218}.$ \n\nAs this is a quadrantal angle, to find the remaining solutions we use our knowledge of the sine function. We know that the sine function has its maximum values at:\n\n$$\nx={90}^{\u2218}+{360}^{\u2218}\\cdot n\n$$\n\nSince the domain ${0}^{\u2218}\\le x<{360}^{\u2218}$ is one period, and we have already found one maximum value in this period, we conclude that $x={90}^{\u2218}$ is the only solution.",
